   "newText": "## Evidence / checks
Two demos confirmed this, including 9×11 → 27×22 and 10×12 → 30×24. In both, the first output row is the last input row horizontally reflected then appended to itself; output dimensions and all rows follow the formula. It solved test task-8016b17b (9×15 → 27×30).

A second recurrence test was accepted on 2026-10-22 (input 7×4, output 21×8):
Input:
`[[4,9,9,6],[4,4,4,6],[4,4,6,4],[1,2,5,1],[4,2,4,2],[4,4,4,4],[2,4,4,6]]`
The accepted result was formed exactly by the rule above: reverse source-row order for the top block, original order for the middle block, reverse order for the bottom block; each row is `row[::-1] + row`. Thus the first output row was `[6,4,4,2,2,4,4,6]`, and output dimensions were 21×8. This confirms applicability to small rectangular grids and diverse colors.

Avoid guessing a plain tiled grid: both axes use reflections/order changes. The vertical block boundaries naturally duplicate some rows because of the palindromic row ordering; retain all three complete blocks."
